# .gitattributes — Vyomanaut V2
#
# Added during the cross-platform hardening pass following M17-E Session
# 17.8.1, before this repo was first checked out on Windows and a Linux
# VM alongside its existing Mac checkout. Without this file, Git's
# platform-dependent autocrlf behavior can silently convert scripts/demo/
# *.sh's line endings to CRLF on a Windows checkout — which then fails
# cryptically under WSL/Git-Bash ("$'\r': command not found", or the
# shebang line failing to resolve at all) — or, in the other direction,
# convert *.ps1 files to LF-only, which some older Windows tooling still
# mishandles. Pinning every text type's line ending explicitly, rather
# than relying on each contributor's own Git config, is the fix.
#
# [REF: build_M17E.md Phase 17.8; cross-platform hardening pass following
# Session 17.8.1]

# Default: normalize everything Git considers text to LF in the
# repository, checked out as LF unless overridden below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts — must stay LF; a CRLF shebang line fails to resolve on
# every POSIX shell (bash, sh, WSL, Git Bash, macOS, Linux).
*.sh    text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts — CRLF is PowerShell's own native convention and
# what Windows-native editors (VS Code on Windows, ISE, Notepad) default
# to; kept distinct from the LF default deliberately, not an oversight.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Go source, SQL migrations, YAML/JSON config, and Markdown docs — always
# LF, regardless of which machine last edited them. golangci-lint and
# gofmt are also line-ending-sensitive in ways that make mixed endings a
# needless source of diff noise across this project's three-machine fleet.
*.go    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.mod   text eol=lf
*.sum   text eol=lf

# Binary — never touched by line-ending normalization regardless of
# extension-based guessing.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.zip   binary
*.exe   binary
